To determine if the vector field {eq}G{/eq} is conservative, we need to calculate its curl:

{eq}\nabla \times \mathbf G = \begin{vmatrix}
\hat i & \hat j & \hat k \\
\frac{\partial}{\partial x} & \frac{\partial}{\partial y} & \frac{\partial}{\partial z} \\
\cos x + y^2 & 2xy - \sin y & 0
\end{vmatrix} = 0\hat i - 0\hat j + (0 - 0)\hat k = 0{/eq}

Since the curl of the vector field is zero, it is conservative.

To find a function whose gradient is {eq}G{/eq}, we integrate the components of {eq}G{/eq} with respect to {eq}x{/eq} and {eq}y{/eq}, respectively:

{eq}\begin{align*}
f_x &= \cos x + y^2 \\
f_y &= 2xy - \sin y
\end{align*}

Integrating the first equation with respect to {eq}x{/eq} gives:

{eq}f(x,y) = \sin x + xy^2 + g(y){/eq}

where {eq}g(y){/eq} is an arbitrary function of {eq}y{/

